    "case_summary": "A woman in her 50s with a medical history of hypertension and depression presented with a 1.5-year history of painful intertriginous wounds. She had been admitted to the hospital multiple times for cellulitis around the wounds, which responded to intravenous antibiotics. She had received a diverting sigmoid colostomy and indwelling urinary catheter to decrease wound contamination. Her wound care required nursing home care. Prior to presentation, she received adalimumab for 3 months without clinical improvement. Family history notable for son with similar condition.\n\nLinear moist, eroded, exophytic, condyloma-like plaques present in:- Bilateral submammary folds- Intergluteal cleft- Under the abdominal apron- Ulcers of the bilateral genitocrural folds- Vulvar edemaThe lips, oropharynx, and ostomy were unremarkable.\n\n1. Histopathology:- Wedge biopsy of submammary plaque showed:  - Pseudoepitheliomatous epidermal hyperplasia  - Focal superficial erosion  - Superficial and deep granulomatous inflammatory infiltrate  - Well-formed, nonnecrotizing granulomas  - Langhans-type multinucleated giant cells  - Epithelioid histiocytes  - Lymphoplasmacytic inflammatory cuff2. Laboratory Tests:- Histochemical stains: negative- Tissue cultures for bacteria, mycobacteria, and fungus: negative- PCR for herpes simplex virus: negative- Direct immunofluorescence studies: negative- PROMETHEUS IBD sgi Diagnostic test results:  - Elevated immunoglobulin G antibodies  - Flagellins: Fla-X 53.5 EU/mL (normal < 36)  - A4-Fla2 50.6 EU/mL (normal < 32.4)3. Imaging Studies:- Computed tomography enterography: unremarkable- Upper endoscopy: unremarkable- Colonoscopy: unremarkableImages included:- Figure A: Clinical photograph of submammary representative linear condyloma-like ulcerated plaque- Figure B: Clinical photograph of linear ulcers of the genitocrural folds- Figures C and D: Hematoxylin-eosin stain of biopsy specimen from submammary plaque",
